#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302360 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca6398a1-b09b-5fe5-8b22-062bcee1ef3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300971}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300971} :answer-field/id #uuid "62bfcebf-053f-576b-a004-657c68248546" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301544} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "140964c7-df0e-5db6-94ce-6a0240be4dfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300040}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300040} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6439ef2-f460-56da-9ba7-f298fa601bbf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c61885c3-83e2-5a8d-b365-c4d1e934aa7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-327847" :question/problem "![](images/ab/ab9ef90cf863fee6776667a967d5939ba3564564d5d7626e07aaa868baea6a87.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-2,1),$ with $a=4$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we again interpret $\\theta $ as the central angle measured from the horizontal line through the center $(h,k).$ On the quarter-arc where both $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\ge 0$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\ge 0$ (moving from the rightmost point to the topmost point), the parameter must satisfy $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\n![](images/df/df6c12657a6a487f93ec3a6213be8ea16e68d363001bdf0ddb8ff68b5201a8c4.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eecdf539-f4c9-5e2a-b1d3-147515693798" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301145}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301145} :answer-field/id #uuid "908f284e-1908-5bfa-9308-7074a7ede550" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301687} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc4981ef-e57a-51b9-b310-d03c74ee1689" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301053}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301053} :answer-field/id #uuid "e34a02c9-d0c9-59ab-b604-48db38e67691" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2e29b9a7-ba3d-59be-8f85-186fc6a70498" :question/math-academy-id "q-328327" :question/problem "![](images/52/5234672d8176a0a04bbc3feecfe13092ff116a25682ae055286f1fe858b8a754.png)\n\nThe half-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-1,2),$ $a=5,$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a half-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the horizontal line through its center.\n\n![](images/1b/1b2d7617af37197dfd2702b4497263e563458b0bf11c622308220def93b688a9.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}],$ we get the half-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the half-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319144 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18e98c30-1d43-5f8a-b696-0c34179f4a2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317642}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317642} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2fe00d1-0029-52ff-8d78-f242bb9ada4e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d61aaced-d2d7-58f8-932d-53be360378fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-346870" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}2x{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{x} & ⇒ & v={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 2x\\cdot {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =2x\\cdot {e}^{x}-\\int {e}^{x}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =2x{e}^{x}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2x{e}^{x}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}-2{e}^{x}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2(1\\cdot {e}^{1}-0\\cdot {e}^{0})-2({e}^{1}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =2e-2(e-1) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319330 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c377238f-b153-585f-8aa9-69ae456b7747" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9{e}^{4}+3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318212}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318212} :answer-field/id #uuid "f381b13c-2394-583e-8bf6-dd424644547a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cb6faac0-b896-57ad-9d54-07d07059068d" :question/math-academy-id "q-349217" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}3x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=3x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=3 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{2x} & ⇒ & v=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 3x\\cdot {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}-\\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}x{e}^{2x}\\,{|}_{0}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}x{e}^{2x}\\,{|}_{0}^{2}-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{2x}\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(2\\cdot {e}^{4}-0\\cdot {e}^{0})-\\frac{3}{4}({e}^{4}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{4}-\\frac{3}{4}({e}^{4}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{9{e}^{4}+3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319331 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4abe39b1-1acd-5859-bbde-adde947b15bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317810}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317810} :answer-field/id #uuid "ceb951fd-f6ee-51b4-b39a-e0e4ddc059fd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "67ba33c4-ecff-5b0f-91ab-b446a5b2d9d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-349231" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=4x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=4 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x) & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 4x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\\,\\text{d}x & =4x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x))-\\int (-\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x))\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{4}{\\pi }x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{\\pi }x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{4}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{\\pi }(1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0))+\\frac{4}{{\\pi }^{2}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{\\pi }(-1-0)+\\frac{4}{{\\pi }^{2}}(0-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319332 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31043ecf-974a-5de9-96ed-984ebdd8c7d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{8\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317718} {:answer/id #uuid "75760b45-83e4-4520-9122-20448259b235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{8\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674599}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317718} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9d52bf5-cb5b-5fca-b529-dba6cdf38afd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "30f0e38b-4d35-5e3a-af39-84633279075a" :question/math-academy-id "q-349253" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=4x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=4 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 4x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =4x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-\\int (-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{4}{3}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{4}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{4}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}(2\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))+\\frac{4}{9}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}(2\\pi \\cdot 1-0\\cdot 1)+\\frac{4}{9}(0-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319333 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f87a2ec9-3787-50b9-9e55-d6cd2446c049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318389}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318389} :answer-field/id #uuid "3da5149a-42e0-57ab-b867-65aa7f203c05" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "615dee59-7992-5c1c-8fe1-9c3970b38307" :question/math-academy-id "q-349297" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}9x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=9x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=9 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & ⇒ & v=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 9x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =9x\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\int \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\cdot 9\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}9x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3}-3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot 0-0\\cdot 0)+(-1-1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319334 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f52ada2e-b967-5581-b835-fdd4ef42d1b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318377}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318377} :answer-field/id #uuid "43a2be7d-5b56-578e-b8ea-825b72904689" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dfc2da83-70ec-5b4d-8276-3941e4e2e19f" :question/math-academy-id "q-349305" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}(-18x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=-18x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=-18 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & ⇒ & v=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (-18x)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(-18x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\int \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\cdot (-18)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}(-18x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3}+6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-6(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-6(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot 0-0\\cdot 0)-2(-1-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319335 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9137fb31-7c6a-5b4c-a54b-cf399bcfa5a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-4{e}^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318059}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318059} :answer-field/id #uuid "c49e403e-dc95-5927-91fe-0a0152fb1039" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fce587bb-2230-5419-afb1-775f458fc405" :question/math-academy-id "q-349348" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}9x{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=9x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=9 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{-3x} & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 9x\\cdot {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =9x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x})-\\int (-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x})\\cdot 9\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}9x{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-3x{e}^{-3x}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}-3{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3x{e}^{-3x}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}-{e}^{-3x}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-3(1\\cdot {e}^{-3}-0\\cdot {e}^{0})-({e}^{-3}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3}-({e}^{-3}-1) \\\\  & =1-4{e}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323711 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "359742a6-8a0e-5705-b305-bdbfab09ad97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2(x+1)^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321557} {:answer/id #uuid "b8e8e21f-dd05-54d4-9135-759212c11a07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+1)^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322582} {:answer/id #uuid "baf21348-aa27-59ef-b997-7967d7ce27a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2y(x+1)^{2y-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322597} {:answer/id #uuid "c728a1b6-53de-52b8-9756-83f9687e51da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{2}(x+1)^{{y}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322691} {:answer/id #uuid "d6641433-9b61-5746-a0f6-b80a7538be32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(x+1)^{{y}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322819}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322691} :answer-field/id #uuid "e72f75b7-2611-5c69-b6cd-8d8554967419" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9932dc44-faa7-51c4-b14b-b1d882efadd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-35713" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ for $f(x,y)=(x+1)^{{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the chain rule: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x+1)^{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}(x+1)^{{y}^{2}-1}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the sum rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}(x+1)^{{y}^{2}-1}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+1) & ={y}^{2}(x+1)^{{y}^{2}-1}\\cdot (\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}(x+1)^{{y}^{2}-1}\\cdot (1+0) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}(x+1)^{{y}^{2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323712 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "206738b8-5b84-547e-9fc3-5c5d89f2d22c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡z+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321393} {:answer/id #uuid "22f21454-5045-5e7c-9bd3-5be290d34f9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡z-(z+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321413} {:answer/id #uuid "247c8ed0-8058-5736-99fc-d402a62c5d95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡z+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321429} {:answer/id #uuid "46f5a4e6-b0ae-5172-87cf-4dfb5cdbd0f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321706} {:answer/id #uuid "afe19fe2-9ce8-59b2-84b2-a3c39bdb418d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322526}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322526} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f75b94c-9bc5-56e1-980b-18e918de3927" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "233491ec-5789-55b3-aaad-7e685a7ed619" :question/math-academy-id "q-35721" :question/problem "Find ${g}_{z}(x,y,z)$ for $g(x,y,z)=(x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡z+(z+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $z,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the sum and constant multiple rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}((x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡z+(z+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}((x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}((z+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =(x+y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡z)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}(z+y) \\\\  & =(x+y)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡z+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =(x+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323732 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09053cf6-741f-589d-be34-d2a1b6174a2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321218} {:answer/id #uuid "4518ed82-7e07-5dd3-ac80-6f7f294b2a4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321695} {:answer/id #uuid "4c778daa-2aae-56ec-994c-19cb5cc2cae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321748} {:answer/id #uuid "76d49207-26c4-56ac-a485-a6f407cdb31d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322072} {:answer/id #uuid "88032f36-3eb2-5a47-a909-5e2c7d37b4a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322230}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322072} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f544854-0876-5f69-aa6b-c91df359ae81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6c3de3bc-c5ed-54c9-b107-f7edd673c56e" :question/math-academy-id "q-35956" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+xy-{y}^{2}}{z{x}^{2}-z{y}^{2}}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Unfortunately, this limit takes the indeterminate form because the numerator and denominator both evaluate to zero at the given point:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+xy-{y}^{2}}{z{x}^{2}-z{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{2}+1\\cdot (-1)-(-1)^{2}}{1\\cdot {1}^{2}-1\\cdot (-1)^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+xy-{y}^{2}}{z{x}^{2}-z{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+2xy-xy-{y}^{2}}{z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2x(x+y)-y(x+y)}{z(x+y)(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{(2x-y)(x+y)}{z(x+y)(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2x-y}{z(x-y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2x-y}{z(x-y)} & =\\frac{2(1)-(-1)}{1(1-(-1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{3}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323738 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3571c649-0531-50fb-81be-aa0dc6824b5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\pi ,\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321556} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff57b68-3c51-53bb-b23d-d256805ec32e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321773} {:answer/id #uuid "a280209e-a266-5259-8e3c-561a3617e91a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨2\\pi ,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322429} {:answer/id #uuid "dec9e1b5-c792-5421-b9a9-29ad4b32bf0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\pi ,\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322888} {:answer/id #uuid "f61c2261-0bcc-57ad-8951-891f80c64105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323075}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322888} :answer-field/id #uuid "81886d51-1e5e-5e76-8e7d-103ef37f4bdb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1ae928cb-8fc1-5d80-9fc4-2442ff5c0221" :question/math-academy-id "q-36005" :question/problem "Evaluate $∇h(x,y)$ at $(1,1)$ if $h(x,y)=4x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y) \\\\  & =\\frac{4x}{{y}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇h(x,y) & =⟨{h}_{x}(x,y),\\,{h}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y,\\,\\frac{4x}{{y}^{2}+1}⟩ \\\\ ∇h(1,1) & =⟨4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1,\\,\\frac{4\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{4}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\pi ,\\,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323740 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f9fc2f6-55bd-579f-8d29-1c23432bc9d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨4,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321268} {:answer/id #uuid "60e28288-b23d-5dcd-b0df-f485ee49c892" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321907} {:answer/id #uuid "67ff6316-9900-5d46-835b-dc3f73fa9490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨8,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321959} {:answer/id #uuid "8b7eefa4-73e4-5852-8337-87a7151461a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322262} {:answer/id #uuid "e6558f18-e9d0-5aff-84ab-a80892c690b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨8,\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322950}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321268} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c408a79-7d5b-5c64-bf80-a6ec1301f01a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4dc17c5e-dc05-517c-b33a-803dd069587a" :question/math-academy-id "q-36009" :question/problem "Evaluate the gradient of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},0)$ if $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),\\,{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟨x,\\,y⟩ \\\\ ∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\,0) & =\\frac{2}{{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{0}^{2}}⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\,\\frac{1}{4}}⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =8⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323752 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5003a38c-04a4-5836-8966-aa51b40a5742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321774} {:answer/id #uuid "9a78d252-aa58-5541-9491-1c5625863789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322373} {:answer/id #uuid "a56d86fa-d11f-5d28-87f6-a135179a191b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322445} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ebe706-b739-54ea-a7f7-8097ef2e52ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322922} {:answer/id #uuid "e2f20116-344c-5a96-91a8-63d897848e44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322923}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322922} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8202b9f-8756-5c9e-8ce9-e8e73b40cad5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323626}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bb26a78b-1c42-5b21-af43-2a1b185bc53e" :question/math-academy-id "q-36108" :question/problem "$\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+yz+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy+xz-yz}=$" :question/worked-solution "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+yz+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy+xz-yz}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(-1,1,0)$ does not cause the denominator to become zero, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-1,1,0)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+yz+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy+xz-yz} & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}+(1)(0)+(0)^{2}}{(-1)^{2}-(-1)(1)+(-1)(0)-(1)(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+0+0}{1+1+0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323753 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5493b42d-db22-5575-bb2f-f6c8fc5d6eaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321811} {:answer/id #uuid "558ecdfc-8fe6-5e98-a11d-9431a5f8ca70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321824} {:answer/id #uuid "db11aabe-6fb7-5f73-bda7-0a282cd8baf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322863} {:answer/id #uuid "f53896e5-4b71-5c33-b0fd-046da56c652b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323070} {:answer/id #uuid "fdbdb260-48f2-5b5b-b5cd-96873b9883f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323148}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321811} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c09b5db-1f6a-58bf-8c80-c3937e3dcaaf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9ae648fa-9940-58c2-8b0b-e34933c46f7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-36109" :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy-2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-xy}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy-2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-xy}=\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{2}+3\\cdot 1\\cdot 2-2\\cdot {2}^{2}}{2\\cdot {1}^{2}-1\\cdot 2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first factoring the denominator, and then canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy-2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-xy} & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+4xy-xy-2{y}^{2}}{x(2x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2x(x+2y)-y(x+2y)}{x(2x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{(2x-y)(x+2y)}{x(2x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{x+2y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{x+2y}{x} & =\\frac{1+2\\cdot 2}{1} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323794 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "478e5238-b82e-52a7-b916-e3176ac5c7a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321709} {:answer/id #uuid "64e34a4f-d3ec-50b3-936b-5c2fc27ddda9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321930} {:answer/id #uuid "b7c06b6f-03ce-5422-811e-bdf364365b2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322577} {:answer/id #uuid "cd17be78-1f08-5dc6-b596-77159cdc94ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322743} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f19b61-a2d2-5713-a47b-f64a9abfebff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322974}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322974} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9d79ebc-11b5-5629-a6fc-dd3e1f57e714" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323599}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "51e044b0-3c0e-5644-831b-0d5fd92a4af2" :question/math-academy-id "q-36346" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point  where $(x,y)=(1,1)$, can be found by computing $∇g(1,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & ={g}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{g}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot y\\,i+\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot x\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,i+\\frac{1}{y}\\,j \\\\ ∇g(1,1) & =\\frac{1}{(1)}\\,i+\\frac{1}{(1)}\\,j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇g(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇g(1,1)}{‖∇g(1,1)‖} & =\\frac{i+j}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323795 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e39d31e-75b0-5793-8278-bebced97730a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-20x-7y+181=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321509} {:answer/id #uuid "372699ce-ec89-581d-9797-1841d9f9de0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-20x+7y+181=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321567} {:answer/id #uuid "8825796b-037c-5e7f-8b8b-68d0db08f793" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-20x+7y+28=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322231} {:answer/id #uuid "90a9aaa5-ef19-541c-89c7-ebbd8c1de0cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20x+7y-139=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322294} {:answer/id #uuid "f052fdc2-234a-5694-9837-38ef76630d26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20x+7y+181=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323040}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321567} :answer-field/id #uuid "a144aff6-eb9c-59ae-8c19-7bfb3a8dd26b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "66ea4a99-e9fe-52c8-93b3-5bc45407e7da" :question/math-academy-id "q-36347" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2xy+6{y}^{2}=x-2.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(8,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy+6{y}^{2} & =x-2 \\\\ 2xy+6{y}^{2}-x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2xy+6{y}^{2}-x$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-2.$   \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(8,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(8,-3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(8,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy+6{y}^{2}-x)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+6{y}^{2}-x)\\,j \\\\  & =(2y-1)\\,i+(2x+12y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(8,-3) & =(2(-3)-1)\\,i+(2(8)+12(-3))\\,j \\\\  & =-7\\,i-20\\,j \\\\  & =⟨-7,-20⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Hence, $∇f(8,-3)=⟨-7,-20⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(8,-3).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-8}{-7} & =\\frac{y-(-3)}{-20} \\\\ -20(x-8) & =-7(y+3) \\\\ -20x+7y+160+21 & =0 \\\\ -20x+7y+181 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323860 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25a66e67-c420-5bae-9224-c8f5d4fde075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨4,\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321440} {:answer/id #uuid "47baee24-d042-549a-8151-b2da72dc39a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨2,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321711} {:answer/id #uuid "9abaf78d-69a4-535f-a08b-0a3417313ea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,8⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322375} {:answer/id #uuid "c8af8076-8ba0-5745-9225-6f71fe87a617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨16,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322707} {:answer/id #uuid "cc23bd77-b2ea-5ecd-832e-27ef06e5c5fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨8,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322734}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322734} :answer-field/id #uuid "561a4834-955d-5829-ab93-034d86fd0c74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4b4ba176-5939-5525-9fa4-02ef800efbcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-36909" :question/problem "Evaluate $∇f(x,y)$ at $(2,1)$ if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}\\sqrt{y+3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\sqrt{y+3}) \\\\  & =2x\\sqrt{y+3} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}\\sqrt{y+3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{y+3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x\\sqrt{y+3},\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{y+3}}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =⟨2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{1+3},\\,\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{1+3}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323861 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02d9e603-cd32-5f3b-902b-7e9672467e90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\text{ }i+(y-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}))\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321173} {:answer/id #uuid "34daa0d5-90eb-5a4b-aa2d-75910e6b15b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{y}-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}))\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321551} {:answer/id #uuid "540220bc-b512-5297-ba09-11bb7feac085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{y}+2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321808} {:answer/id #uuid "60d1352a-42b1-5de3-9128-c508fc1e23ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321906} {:answer/id #uuid "6c9d8c76-ca28-5482-a971-dc4ecb87331f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+2y(\\frac{1}{y}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}))\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321992}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321551} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7786cc2-42a8-56f0-a43a-1f962ff29273" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323561}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a48a9c83-f027-5fdb-8704-573396808d2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-36910" :question/problem "Given  $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})$, find $∇g(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot y+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y}-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & ={g}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }i+{g}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{y}-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}))\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323868 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0404f2a9-3d08-5a53-a026-f44a816bf37f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{3}\\text{ }j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321188} {:answer/id #uuid "13134d6f-499b-591c-bde5-f5b16ff56598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }i+\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\text{ }j-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321294} {:answer/id #uuid "39e1fd9f-c905-53ce-88ed-48a994829d3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{6}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321589} {:answer/id #uuid "457eb332-a887-58f4-9e71-5175afe5b10a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\text{ }i+6\\text{ }j-2\\text{ }k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321697} {:answer/id #uuid "6f462375-be9f-527c-9fe5-1e84d47ed3f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{3}\\text{ }j-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322010}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321589} :answer-field/id #uuid "c77c2c1d-e1c1-5a2d-9ca2-89a1901c372b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323562}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5313963c-57a3-55b2-bdc3-c997dcee0402" :question/math-academy-id "q-36969" :question/problem "Evaluate $∇f(x,y,z)$ at the point $(2,3,1)$ if $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{xyz}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{xyz})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot yz \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2x} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{xyz})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot xz \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2y} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{xyz})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot xy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }i+{f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{2y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{2z}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,3,1) & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 2}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 3}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 1}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{6}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323918 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18cbb66c-c87e-532e-b215-79cebdd50220" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2e-8)\\,i+(\\frac{e}{2}-8)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321343} {:answer/id #uuid "25737ab8-5934-517a-82e3-355f226249ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1-4e)\\,i+(\\frac{2}{e}-8)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321438} {:answer/id #uuid "569fd915-b891-5273-a763-b23c030c775d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2-8e)\\,i+(2-8e)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321831} {:answer/id #uuid "91bfacf0-e81a-591f-b5c7-eb843413fffc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{e}-8)i+(8e-2)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322304} {:answer/id #uuid "e6c24a19-a0b8-5781-9f03-f3684c7d7f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(8-2e)\\,i+(\\frac{8}{e}-2)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322954}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322304} :answer-field/id #uuid "2328f82b-ecf4-5e93-897c-028b4fd16484" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "12b276e2-b703-55f7-94b8-069326b3f510" :question/math-academy-id "q-37516" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=8xy+2-8e.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(1,e).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =8xy+2-8e \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy & =2-8e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=2-8e.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,e)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(1,e)={f}_{y}(1,e)\\,i-{f}_{x}(1,e)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{2x}{y}-8x)i-(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8y)\\,j \\\\ t(1,e) & =(\\frac{2(1)}{e}-8(1))i-(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-8(e))\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{e}-8)i+(8e-2)j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $(\\frac{2}{e}-8)i+(8e-2)\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323974 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66ee7868-897f-5ab3-8d7e-e9355eee0531" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321950} {:answer/id #uuid "6ad500fb-8c54-539b-bf4d-cd40316e5887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321981} {:answer/id #uuid "772ad7c2-6121-5b62-96eb-0eca3dab85ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322080} {:answer/id #uuid "b5556dee-a933-508a-b4bb-1dcfb4c54712" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322561} {:answer/id #uuid "de02e7bb-d9a4-5bb9-bf6b-5f3af8e67385" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322882}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322561} :answer-field/id #uuid "95a9de04-2eb7-53e7-9fc6-8703030e5631" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323460}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8bac3242-8499-5338-a15f-874124048b1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-38100" :question/problem "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(32-0)+1(0-0)+2(0-0) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 32 \\\\  & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323975 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39c95b18-19e1-5456-b91d-959e901cf5fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321588} {:answer/id #uuid "443dd17c-f660-5dbb-86c7-79e97787046f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321686} {:answer/id #uuid "9fe1db1b-04fb-517a-b8fb-6e795c914341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322411} {:answer/id #uuid "aa28735a-4d12-597b-bcb9-1a0875584598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322484} {:answer/id #uuid "ecaf6396-e5cd-5350-bf10-b76c299fd393" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323011}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321686} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3dff2be-35f0-5987-b839-5c5e285a7aaf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "78a8b15f-9044-5eb2-9f6b-f2e65c9a7882" :question/math-academy-id "q-38124" :question/problem "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & -4\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & -4\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(4-0)-0+0 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326669 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0635b999-97d3-5d18-9b12-3caeac9cc177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324029} {:answer/id #uuid "348915a7-95ed-5f25-ae7f-358a86044ca7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324396} {:answer/id #uuid "60f0d229-ffe4-53c3-82e2-22709bf924f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324780} {:answer/id #uuid "7662322d-927c-5fb0-b5dc-73ffb2cfb282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324955} {:answer/id #uuid "af6c20f8-fd9a-5e48-9e1e-402c93631c91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325467}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324955} :answer-field/id #uuid "41683691-8fdb-5eeb-ac09-97fa8e332f92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ad125b3e-fcca-54de-89e1-91d2698a7860" :question/math-academy-id "q-38174" :question/problem "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -4\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -4\\end{vmatrix} & =(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1(-20-0)-0+3(0-5) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-20)+3\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =20-15 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326670 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "205ccffc-01c3-5074-bcb8-cc4b974d5c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324240} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb5b773-84ff-5a8d-8b87-3631203e61e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324319} {:answer/id #uuid "822fd678-004a-5da8-9c3e-6a6e50c794ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325066} {:answer/id #uuid "c67d2e1d-8417-5a78-a35c-8205ba48cf31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325685} {:answer/id #uuid "f4ea0889-e7f0-58ed-8cf7-e8e35863a9f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326127}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325685} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4aa40ce-8299-5985-952f-3e95c613ab8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "93db7f8f-7ceb-59cb-bb66-3026f7154ffa" :question/math-academy-id "q-38176" :question/problem "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & =6\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}+7\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6(-2-0)-0+7(0-2) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (-2)+7\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-12-14 \\\\  & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326671 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6ffe837-0ba9-566a-847f-01c50e5b0d4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438254} {:answer/id #uuid "0c851c6e-8e6f-5ce1-85f0-794bba8508bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438255}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438255} :answer-field/id #uuid "ece7019d-4e4a-54c5-8c4b-3ee796bd1176" :answer-field/key "$E$ is a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439156} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69b3cb09-83cc-54f2-9f3a-46d5234e0bc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438256} {:answer/id #uuid "e013cbff-c909-515f-a71d-027cdb4811c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438257}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438256} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bcb1241-521a-5c92-ac65-6398206509ea" :answer-field/key "$E$ is **not**  a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439157} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1785546f-b00e-5a43-be50-0c26a6fc2ca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438258} {:answer/id #uuid "1c201000-e71f-5609-9f1d-19bba5ab4630" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438259}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438258} :answer-field/id #uuid "e28dc200-2ac1-54de-90ea-6e018bd1ba08" :answer-field/key "$det(E)=-2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "084b3fe8-adf9-5ab7-9017-b2d4d829ca62" :question/math-academy-id "q-38179" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $E$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nE=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & 3 & 4 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 5 & 6 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 7 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(E) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & 3 & 4 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 5 & 6 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 7 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 6 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & 6 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 8\\end{vmatrix}+4\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & 5 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(40-42)-3(\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{12}{3})+4(\\frac{14}{3}-\\frac{10}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}+4\\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{12}{3}+\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(E)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement “$E$ is a singular matrix” is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326672 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11f2d595-4c96-599f-b04a-ef1a4facbf7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324134} {:answer/id #uuid "35476165-d1db-5ee5-b183-b3201978c25b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324408} {:answer/id #uuid "36554c2e-a686-5080-983a-7fd2be339db8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324414} {:answer/id #uuid "5dc9de58-8130-508b-ad5a-dc48cfe4914d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324751} {:answer/id #uuid "a45499fc-34c3-5b71-b300-396e74e38e5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325368}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324134} :answer-field/id #uuid "17302b73-c3c5-5ca7-9ed6-b18a09cd2b48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "687c1640-263b-54f0-8cd7-1fc76a39da2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-38180" :question/problem "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0+1(0-6)+3(-2-0) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-6)+3\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-6-6 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326673 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79e4e142-7005-54c1-8d51-b38fccf87ef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438260} {:answer/id #uuid "54cfd4e5-c485-53e9-8b91-e2afca217595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438261}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438260} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc0815ad-f485-5e0c-8048-0318325f0ad4" :answer-field/key "$Q$ is **not** a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439159} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f7c0c3a-e50c-57b3-b6e3-787c897947c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438262} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0b4616-bf0b-52ad-8642-7f8e6022ec10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438263}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438263} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef01e74d-81a9-5880-81c3-faa3592706db" :answer-field/key "$Q$ is a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439160} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ce5156b-be05-52db-8fa2-508a2bebc677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438264} {:answer/id #uuid "5c652fd1-d715-5a76-8fb9-1477a177d25a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438265}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438264} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4c6df6b-8841-5610-8b0d-3ae518f8442e" :answer-field/key "$det(Q)=-5$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f2a72ef8-dad7-5172-8868-8d2781696df8" :question/math-academy-id "q-38182" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $Q$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Q) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 7 \\\\ 5 & 8\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{vmatrix}+6\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-3(8-14)+6(5-8) \\\\  & =0-3\\cdot (-6)+6\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =18-18 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(Q)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement “$Q$ is a singular matrix” is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326759 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "346fd834-9f4b-5298-99e8-684dece5b13b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324393} {:answer/id #uuid "40fc053f-efcc-59ec-af1c-fef223bf76b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324495} {:answer/id #uuid "c90f25cc-5183-51bc-8ea0-dfe66b0a8547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325713} {:answer/id #uuid "cd18a5c9-22f8-510d-b764-46c7b3cf4a87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325748} {:answer/id #uuid "f57bcf97-908f-5723-a9d1-77a0ae528e17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326132}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325748} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0b9a63a-36ef-528c-ae36-20b4778b17db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e4234b69-bc6e-5b88-b148-08ea10478920" :question/math-academy-id "q-39345" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from the point $A(1,2)$ to the point $B(3,4),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+y\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+2\\,j$ and $b=3\\,i+4\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =i+2\\,j+t(3\\,i+4\\,j-(i+2\\,j)) \\\\  & =i+2\\,j+t(2\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =(1+2t)\\,i+(2+2t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=1+2t$ and $y(t)=2+2t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+y\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+2t)\\cdot 2+(2+2t)\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6+8t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[6t+4{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[6(1)+4(1)^{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
